  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>In general, interhalogen compounds are more reactive than halogens (except fluorine). This is because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mo>-</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mo>'</mo></msup></math> bond in interhalogens is weaker than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math> bond in halogens except <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></math> bond. All these undergo hydrolysis giving halide ion derived from the smaller halogen and a hypohalite (when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>XX</mi><mo>'</mo></msup></math>), halite (when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi>XX</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>'</mo></msubsup></math>), halate (when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi>XX</mi><mn>5</mn><mo>'</mo></msubsup></math>) and perhalate&nbsp;(when<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced open=\"\"><msup><msub><mi>XX</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>'</mo></msup></mfenced></math> anion derived from the larger halogen.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>XX</mi><mo>'</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo><msup><mi>HX</mi><mo>'</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>HOX</mi></math></p>",
